 1.1 For two successive rectangles $r_1,r_2$, let $\psi=r_1+r_2$ be their cellwise sum as a two-chain, and let $\mathbf x,\mathbf z$ be the initial and terminal states. The state permutation changes by one transposition at each rectangle. If $\mathbf x\ne\mathbf z$, their relative permutation is either a product of two disjoint transpositions, so four points move, or a $3$-cycle, so three points move. These are the only nonidentity products of two transpositions. [F1, given, algebra]
 
-1.2 In the four-point case, the two transpositions are disjoint and commute. Exchanging their order gives the alternate intermediate state; the first rectangle in one decomposition has the same support as the second rectangle in the other, and conversely. Thus both decompositions have the same two rectangle interiors and are empty together. No third decomposition is possible, because the four distinct changing corners must be paired into the same two disjoint transpositions fixed by the endpoint permutation and the domain boundary. [step 1.1, construct]
+1.2 For a counted rectangle $r$ the interior contains no state point, so [F3] gives $M_{\mathbb O}(\mathbf x)-M_{\mathbb O}(\mathbf y)=1-2n_{\mathbb O}(r)$. The coefficient $\prod_iU_i^{O_i(r)}$ lowers Maslov degree by $2n_{\mathbb O}(r)$ by [F2]; hence the full term has degree $-1$. [F2, F3, algebra]
 
-1.3 In the three-point case, following the boundary of $\psi$ gives a six-corner region with five right-angle corners and one reflex corner of angle $270^\circ$; every cell coefficient is zero or one. At the reflex corner the boundary can be cut in exactly two ways into two rectangles. Each cut gives the required intermediate grid state, and the resulting interiors contain no additional state point because any such point would already lie in the interior of one of the original empty rectangles. The six-corner boundary has no other concave corner at which to make a cut, so these are exactly the two decompositions. [step 1.1, construct]
