-1.5 If $\mathbf x=\mathbf z$, the two rectangles reverse the same state transposition and their union is a periodic annulus. If the annulus were not thin, its transverse width would include at least two grid strips; the interior would then contain a state point on an intervening grid circle, contradicting emptiness. Hence every returning domain is one of the $n$ horizontal thin annuli, each occupying one row strip, or one of the $n$ vertical thin annuli, each occupying one column strip. Conversely, the two state points on the boundary circles of any one-row strip split that strip into two empty rectangles, and the same holds for every one-column strip, so all $2n$ listed annuli occur as returning composites. Each contains exactly one $O$ and one $X$ by the row and column marking conditions, so it is excluded in tilde and associated-graded minus modes. In the full filtered minus mode, the horizontal annulus through the row of $O_i$ contributes $U_i$, and the vertical annulus through its column also contributes $U_i$. Every $O_i$ therefore occurs twice among all returning domains, and the total is zero over $\mathbb F_2$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
